From ae3a64452ce1c0a0a229073814e3d03ed42b5c9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:10:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Find the track shift of a Stanford analysis where its rewind
 cannot be told

The reader put the rewind perforation on the bar's rewind track and
otherwise fell back on a shift of nought, unless the columns spanned
exactly as many positions as the bar has. Across the 457 analyses of
the SUPRA archive that reads 46 on other tracks than Stanford's own
raw MIDI gives them, without a word: most at nought, where a rewind
slot punched in one piece leaves nothing trailing the last attack and
a stray hole or an unpunched outermost position puts the span off the
bar's, and the rest off a stray hole past the end taken for the
rewind. Of the 38 rolls the archive holds twice, seven had their two
copies on different tracks, and such copies do not align.

The rewind is still the landmark, where it leaves the chains on the
bar, give or take a stray. Otherwise the holes say which shifts keep
them on the bar, and where they admit several, the middle of the
paper chooses among them. That agrees with Stanford's raw MIDI on 446
of 455 files, against 409 before, and puts the two copies of 37 of the
38 pairs on the same tracks. The pair left is WM 371, whose analysis
hc909pp9868 holds only the middle 38 columns. Of the nine files still
differing from the raw MIDI, the pairs show two to be files whose raw
MIDI is off itself (vj076nk8220, hp956bx4547).

